{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3162","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3162","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3162\",\"parent_revision\":\"7975dd6b68acf21b739f4b95890471ad0ebad384\",\"root_revision\":\"53f20d50d3d71de818de5665f97168f19fc6828e3f0bb27c38b4ae61d5d8db9a\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: 2026-08-22\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nAgent files across the development-harness and python plugins instruct MCP calls whose parameter shapes do not exist, so an agent following the instruction raises rather than storing its output.\\n\\nFour instances found while removing the plan-file authoring model:\\n\\n- `agents/feature-researcher.md` Step 6 called `sam_plan(action=\\\"update\\\", plan_slug=..., section=..., content=...)`. `UpdatePlanConfig` has no `plan_slug` or `section` parameter, and `dh_core.operations.update_plan_fields` raises `ValueError(\\\"append_section_name requires task_id\\\")` \u2014 there is no plan-level section append at all.\\n- `agents/codebase-analyzer.md` carried the same call as its Strategy B.\\n- `plugins/python-engineering/agents/python-cli-design-spec.md` and its duplicate in `python3-development` instructed `sam_create` and `sam_update`, names absent from the SAM surface, which exposes plan, task, and active-task operations only.\\n\\nThose four are corrected. One remains and cannot be corrected the same way:\\n\\n- `agents/ecosystem-researcher.md` Step 5 uses the same non-existent `sam_plan update` shape. Its `tools:` frontmatter declares no backlog MCP tools and it does not load the artifact-creation skill, so it cannot reach `artifact_register` either. Correcting the call requires a frontmatter change to grant the operations, not only a prose change.\\n\\nCommon shape: the instruction names a real tool with invented parameters. It reads as correct to an author and to a reviewer because the tool exists; only calling it reveals the shape is wrong. Nothing validates an agent file's tool invocations against the servers' actual signatures.\\n\\nImpact: the agent's output is not stored. Whether the failure is visible depends on how the agent handles the exception, and a research or analysis stage that raises after doing its work loses the work.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 2026-08-23, while removing the plan-file authoring model\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-22\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Chosen Approach\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nFix the runtime, and add narrow guards rather than a general parser.\\n\\nRuntime: `extra=\\\"forbid\\\"` on `_ActionConfigBase`; enum-typed `artifact_type` and `status` on `artifact_register`; raise rather than return an error envelope in the artifact handlers. This rejects a wrong call at every site, including the ones no parser can reach. One risk to verify first: `sam_schema/sam_plan.py` builds `CreatePlanConfig.model_validate({...})` from CLI input \u2014 confirm it passes no extras before forbidding them.\\n\\nGuards: three targeted tests modelled on the existing section-registry drift guard \u2014 frontmatter tool grants scoped to the dh prefixes, enum literals, and tool-name existence. Measured zero false positives across 1571 shipped files, and they find 19 real defects.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Why The General Parser Was Rejected\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nIt was built and run rather than argued about. Across 1571 shipped files there are 548 call-shaped sites; 78.5% parse into a call with keyword arguments. Only 20 of the 548 sit in a `python` fence \u2014 280 are loose prose, 124 in `text` fences, 75 in `mermaid`, so fence-language gating is not viable. Running it produced 98 flags: 64 genuine, 34 false positives at 34.7%, all from one pattern (prose naming an operation).\\n\\nThe decisive evidence is a false negative. The first probe used a word-boundary anchor before the tool name; a word boundary cannot match after an underscore, so every one of the 120 `mcp__plugin_dh_\u2026__`-prefixed sites \u2014 22% of the corpus \u2014 was skipped silently, and the probe reported a healthy-looking 62 flags. That is precisely the failure mode of the artifact-ownership guard added earlier: a guard that passes while the defect it targets is live. A general parser also remains structurally blind to frontmatter grants, mermaid nodes, CLI-form invocations, and the entire semantic class.\\n\\nCLI-form invocations need no guard: Typer exits non-zero on an unknown flag, so they already fail loudly.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Open Decision\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nWhether `skills/backlog/CLI_TO_MCP_MIGRATION.md` and `backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md` are live instructions or historical records. They hold 16 or more of the flags. Forcing corrections into a historical audit document falsifies the record, and is the fastest route to the guard being switched off.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Correction The Counts Above Are Wrong Two Of The Claims Are False And The Defect Is Not In Markdown\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAn adversarial design review checked every claim in this issue against source. Three corrections, all verified independently.\\n\\n**`task-plan` is real.** `ArtifactType.TASK_PLAN` exists in `backlog_core/models.py`, and `sam_schema/core/artifact_registry_client.py` registers a `TASK_PLAN` manifest entry on plan store. The reads are not invented and must not be swept \u2014 they return empty only under a content backend without the gist task layer. The claim above that nothing registers this type is false.\\n\\n**`groomed_content` is real.** It is a parameter of `backlog_core.operations.update_item` and `groom_item`. It is absent from the MCP tool surface, not from the system. Related: `skills/backlog/README.md` asserts it is \\\"MCP-only\\\", which is the inverse of the truth.\\n\\n**The counts are inflated.** Actual figures within the plugin are 10 / 6 / 7 / 4 / 26, not 19 / 45 / 21 / 8 / 104. The larger numbers included the `.claude/backlog/` local cache, which is not shipped content.\\n\\n**The defect is in two source files, not in markdown.** The instructed calls are wrong because the runtime accepts them:\\n\\n- `_ActionConfigBase` in `sam_schema/core/action_models.py` sets only `populate_by_name=True`. The tool's top-level schema emits `additionalProperties: false`, but the config branches emit nothing. That omission is the entire silent-ignore class \u2014 an unknown key on a config is dropped and the call reports success having written nothing.\\n- `artifact_register` in `backlog_core/server.py` declares `artifact_type: str` and `status: str` rather than the enums, so the schema advertises no allowed values, and an invalid literal raises inside a handler whose `except (ValueError, KeyError)` converts it into a success envelope containing an `error` key. That handler pattern occurs 52 times in that file and contradicts the error contract documented for the SAM server, where exceptions propagate and the framework converts them to an error response.\\n\\nMarkdown is where the symptom is visible. Correcting markdown alone leaves the mechanism that permits it.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Also Found\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nEight live `sam_create` / `sam_update` calls remain \u2014 both `python-cli-design-spec` copies and `python-engineering/agents/code-reviewer.md`. Sixteen wrong `backlog_close` / `backlog_resolve` shapes in `skills/backlog/CLI_TO_MCP_MIGRATION.md`. `docs/workflow-layers/KNOWN_ENTITIES.md` states a tool count that disagrees with the live server and omits a tool four frontmatters grant; it should be generated from the server's tool listing rather than hand-maintained.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Validation\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA known-defect fixture is mandatory, and is what the prior guard lacked: freeze today's genuine flags as a fixture the guard must continue to flag, plus mutation tests injecting each defect class into a temporary copy. Then red-first tests; then live `fastmcp call` against both servers with the exact wrong shapes, confirming the response envelope changes from success-containing-an-error-key to a true error; then run the T0/TN pair against a scratch item and read the baseline back, which returns nothing today and is the reproduction.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measured Scope And Why These Fail Silently\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nCounts on `main`, excluding generated graph output:\\n\\n| Pattern | Occurrences | Why it fails |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `status=\\\"complete\\\"` | 19 | `ArtifactStatus` is `draft`/`current`/`superseded`/`archived`. Constructing it from `\\\"complete\\\"` raises, so the registration does not happen. |\\n| `artifact_read(..., \\\"task-plan\\\")` | 45 | Nothing anywhere registers a `task-plan` artifact, so the read returns nothing every time. |\\n| `plan_slug` / `section` / `content` on the plan update action | 21 | `UpdatePlanConfig` accepts `append_section_name`, `section_content`, `task_id`. The model ignores unknown keys, so the call succeeds and writes nothing. |\\n| `artifact_migrate` in `tools:` | 8 | No such tool on the server. The entry is dropped and the rest of the grant survives. |\\n| `backlog_update(groomed_content=)` | 104 | No such parameter. |\\n\\nThree distinct silent-failure mechanisms, none of which surfaces to the calling agent:\\n\\n- An unknown key on a Pydantic config is ignored, so a malformed call returns success having written nothing.\\n- An invalid enum value raises inside the handler and is returned as an error payload rather than propagating, so an agent that does not inspect the result continues as though it succeeded.\\n- A read for an artifact type nothing produces returns empty, which is indistinguishable from a legitimate absence.\\n\\nThe affected agents include `t0-baseline-capture` and `tn-verification-gate`, whose artifacts the completion gate compares against each other. A baseline that was never registered and a verification that reads a type nothing produces cannot detect a regression.\\n\\nNothing in the test suite validates an agent file's instructed tool calls against the servers' actual parameter models, which is why a migration pass could introduce a whole parameter shape that never existed and leave no failing test.\\n\\nPartial remediation has landed where these patterns sat inside files being corrected for other reasons; the counts above are what remains on `main`. A targeted sweep is needed rather than incidental correction, because the failure is invisible at the call site and the reader cannot tell a working instruction from a broken one.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"9bb353b028608a44e469a40f63f9c5e8bcb2c9d7b0221da4c83a254a87883a15\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABRRdg1Q\"}"}